            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">CNG Price in Haryana — IGL Extension, Delhi-Adjacent Rates</h3>
            <p class="text-body mb-6">
              Haryana's CNG network is largely an extension of Delhi's IGL grid into Gurugram, Faridabad and Panipat, which means the <strong>CNG price in Haryana</strong> for those cities typically tracks Delhi's within a narrow band — the same distributor, the same gas sourcing, the same compression economics, with only the state's tax layer creating any gap. Haryana's VAT structure (18.2% plus additional tax on petrol; CNG-specific rates differ) applies where relevant, but the network efficiency of sharing IGL's infrastructure keeps per-kg rates from straying far from the capital. Outside the NCR belt, CNG availability in Haryana thins out — Hisar, Karnal and Rohtak have coverage in varying degrees as the network expands. <a href="/cng-price-in-haryana/" class="text-primary hover:underline font-semibold">Haryana CNG price page →</a>
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">CNG Price in Punjab — Tricity Belt, Narrower Coverage</h3>
            <p class="text-body mb-6">
              The <strong>CNG price in Punjab</strong> is most relevant around the Chandigarh–Mohali–Panchkula belt, where CGD coverage is most mature. Punjab's tax structure — 16.58% plus additional levies on petrol — has a CNG-specific counterpart wherever VAT applies, and the state's position on the gas pipeline network keeps supply costs reasonable relative to hill states to the north. Beyond the Tricity and Ludhiana–Jalandhar corridor, CNG availability across Punjab is still patchy compared to Delhi NCR or Maharashtra, so the state average matters less here than the specific city rate. <a href="/cng-price-in-punjab/" class="text-primary hover:underline font-semibold">Punjab CNG price page →</a>
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">CNG Price in UP — Lucknow, Kanpur, Agra & the NCR Fringe</h3>
            <p class="text-body mb-6">
              Uttar Pradesh has one of India's expanding CNG footprints — IGL's network runs deep into the state, covering <strong>CNG price in Lucknow</strong> ({lucknowCng ?? "tracked here"}), Kanpur, Agra, Varanasi, and the NCR fringe cities of Ghaziabad and Noida. UP's tax structure (19.36% or ₹14.85/L floor on petrol, with CNG-specific VAT where charged) keeps rates in a moderate band. The practical map for UP drivers: NCR cities track Delhi's IGL pricing; Lucknow and Kanpur have their own distributor economics based on gas sourcing distance; Agra benefits from Mathura-refinery-adjacent infrastructure. Coverage is expanding west to east, but eastern UP districts still have thinner access than the Lucknow–Kanpur–Agra triangle. <a href="/cng-price-in-uttar-pradesh/" class="text-primary hover:underline font-semibold">UP CNG price page →</a>
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">CNG Price in Kerala — Limited Network, High-Tax Environment</h3>
            <p class="text-body mb-6">
              The <strong>CNG price in Kerala</strong> is a different story from North and West India — not because the pricing formula differs, but because the network itself is still limited. Kerala's CNG availability is concentrated in pilot areas around Kochi and Thiruvananthapuram, with the state's broader CGD build-out still in progress. Where CNG is sold, Kerala's tax environment applies — the same stacked VAT-and-cess structure that puts the state's petrol among India's most expensive — which means per-kg rates in Kerala don't get the low-tax tailwind that CNG enjoys in, say, Gujarat or Chandigarh. For most Kerala drivers, the CNG conversion decision currently hinges more on station availability than on per-kg price. <a href="/cng-price-in-kerala/" class="text-primary hover:underline font-semibold">Kerala CNG price page →</a>
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-ink mb-6">CNG vs Petrol — The Per-Kg Savings Math</h2>
            <p class="text-body mb-6">
              The reason most people care about the <strong>CNG price per kg</strong> in the first place is the cost-per-kilometre gap against petrol. CNG typically delivers a noticeably lower running cost per km for two reasons: the per-kg price is usually well below the per-litre petrol price in the same city, and CNG engines often run more kilometres per unit of fuel energy in city driving. The exact gap depends on your vehicle's mileage on both fuels, the local price spread, and how you drive — which is why a rough rule of thumb ("CNG is half the cost") is less useful than running the actual numbers for your car.
            </p>
            <p class="text-body mb-6">
              If you're weighing a conversion or a new CNG vehicle, take today's rate from your city's page above, multiply by your expected CNG mileage, and compare it against your current petrol cost using our <a href="/calculator" class="text-primary hover:underline font-semibold">fuel cost calculator</a>. The <a href="/compare" class="text-primary hover:underline font-semibold">compare tool</a> also lines up petrol, diesel, CNG and autogas side by side for any tracked city, so you can see the spread without doing the arithmetic yourself. The general pattern across India's major networks: CNG wins clearly on running cost where station density makes refuelling convenient; the calculus gets tighter in cities where you'll be routing around for stations.
            </p>

